TACACS+ vs RADIUS (Simple + Exam Ready)
First — what they BOTH do
Both are used to manage AAA:
•	Authentication → Who are you?
•	Authorization → What can you access?
•	Accounting → What did you do?
They’re commonly used for:
•	Network logins
•	VPN access
•	Admin access to routers/switches
•	Enterprise environments
•	RADIUS:
Centralized AAA for user network access (WiFi/VPN).
•	TACACS+:
Centralized AAA for administrative access to network devices.
 In the context of the AAA framework, common methods for authenticating people include: (Username and password, Biometrics, MFA) 
In a Zero Trust architecture:
•	Control Plane → defines, manages, and makes decisions about security policies (who can access what, under what conditions).
•	Data Plane → enforces those policies by allowing/denying traffic and access based on the Control Plane’s decisions.
• Policy Decision Point (PDP) → Control Plane
•	This is the “brain”
•	It decides if access should be allowed or denied
•	It creates/defines the policy decisions
• Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) → Data Plane
•	This is the “gatekeeper”
•	It allows or blocks the traffic based on what the PDP decided
•	•  PDP = Decide
•	•  PEP = Enforce
Honeytoken:  A unique identifier that is designed to track attackers
•  Honeyfile → a decoy file meant to attract attackers
•	Example: a fake “passwords.xlsx” placed in a shared folder
•  Honeypot → a decoy system/network used to lure attackers
•  Honeytoken → a hidden marker/identifier used to detect and track misuse
•	Example:
o	A fake credential
o	A fake API key
o	A unique tracking link
o	A planted database record
PKI (Public Key Infrastructure) ✅
This is the entire system that handles:
•	Creating certificates
•	Managing them
•	Storing them
•	Distributing them
•	Revoking them
Data encryption is the typical use of public key. 
•  Key escrow = storing a copy of encryption keys with a trusted third party so data can be recovered later.
•  Recovery Agent (RA) = a person/system that is authorized to help recover encrypted data.
An MS Windows component that enables encryption of individual files is called: EFS
EFS ✅
•	Built into Windows
•	Encrypts individual files and folders
•	You can right-click a file → Properties → Encrypt
•	SED (Self-Encrypting Drive) 
o	Hardware-based encryption built into the drive itself
Quick memory trick for Security+:
•	EFS = File-level encryption
•	BitLocker = Disk-level encryption
PGP (Pretty Good Privacy)
•	The original encryption program created by Phil Zimmermann
•	Used for:
o	Encrypting emails
o	Encrypting files
o	Digital signatures
•	Uses public-key cryptography (you have a public key + private key)
Think of it as the original technology.
GPG (GNU Privacy Guard)
•	An open-source implementation of PGP
•	Does the same core things:
o	Encrypt/decrypt data
o	Sign/verify messages
•	Based on the OpenPGP standard
•	Free and widely used on Linux and in security tools
Think of it as the modern, free version of PGP.
Quick Security+ way to remember
•	PGP = the concept/original software
•	GPG = the open-source tool that implements PGP standards
What they’re used for (exam focus)
Both are used for:
•	Email encryption
•	File encryption
•	Authentication via digital signatures
•	Protecting data in transit and at rest
 Deprecated TLS-based method for secure transmission of email messages
SMTPS 
SMTPS ✅
•	This was the old method of securing email using SSL/TLS by default on port 465
•	It’s now considered deprecated/legacy
•	Modern systems use STARTTLS instead
Easy way to remember:
•	SMTPS = old, implicit TLS → deprecated
•	STARTTLS = modern, upgrade to TLS → current standard
Obsolete protocol used for secure data transfer over the web: SHTTP
